.TH BMAP-SEND 1 "2026-11-11" "bmaptool" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bmap-send \- send only the mapped blocks of an image to a remote host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bmap-send
[\-P \fIPORT\fR] [\-y] [\-\-full] [\-\-zero-holes]
[\-\-remote-python \fIPYTHON\fR]
[\-\-verify | \-\-no-verify] \fIIMAGE\fR \fIDESTINATION\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fIbmap-send\fR sends an image and its bmap file to a remote host. By default it
reads the mapped block ranges from the bmap file and transfers only their data
over SSH, so holes in a sparse image are never sent. The remote side runs a
small Python 3 program which writes every range at its original offset.
.PP
The bmap file must be generated beforehand with \fIbmaptool create\fR. Locally
it is expected as \fIIMAGE\fR.bmap. On the remote side it is stored next to the
image as \fIDESTINATION\fR.bmap, so that it can be verified there with
\fIbmaptool verify\fR.
.PP
\fIDESTINATION\fR has the form [user@]host:/path/to/image.raw. If the remote
path ends with "/", it is treated as a directory and the local image name is
appended. The remote bmap file is stored next to the image as
/path/to/image.raw.bmap.
.PP
The remote side needs a Python 3 interpreter to run the receiver. The default is
\fBpython3\fR; use \fB\-\-remote-python\fR to select another interpreter. Use
\fB\-\-full\fR to fall back to the old behaviour of sending the whole image with
\fBscp\fR, in which case no remote Python is needed.
.PP
When the destination is a block device, the holes keep whatever data the device
already contains. Use \fB\-\-zero-holes\fR to write zeroes to the holes; the
zeroes are generated on the remote side and are not transferred over the
network. Regular files are truncated to the image size, so their holes read
back as zero without this option.
.PP
By default the remote copy is not verified. Use \fB\-\-verify\fR to run
\fIbmaptool verify\fR on the remote host after the transfer. The remote host
must have \fIbmaptool\fR installed for this option.
.PP
If the source image modification time differs from the time recorded in the
bmap, \fIbmap-send\fR prints a warning and asks "Continue? [y/N]". Only "y" or
"yes" continues; any other answer or end of input aborts the transfer. This
catches an image that was modified after its bmap was created.
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
\-h, \-\-help
.RS 2
Print short help text and exit.
.RE
.PP
\-P, \-\-port \fIPORT\fR
.RS 2
Use \fIPORT\fR for the SSH connection.
.RE
.PP
\-y, \-\-yes
.RS 2
Continue without prompting when the source image mtime differs from the bmap.
.RE
.PP
\-\-full
.RS 2
Send the whole image with \fBscp\fR instead of only the mapped ranges. This is
the behaviour of older \fIbmap-send\fR versions and is useful when the remote
side cannot run the Python receiver.
.RE
.PP
\-\-zero-holes
.RS 2
When the destination is a block device, write zeroes to the holes. The zeroes
are generated on the remote side; only the mapped ranges are transferred.
.RE
.PP
\-\-remote-python \fIPYTHON\fR
.RS 2
Python 3 interpreter used on the remote side (default: \fBpython3\fR).
.RE
.PP
\-\-verify
.RS 2
Run \fIbmaptool verify\fR on the remote host after the transfer. The remote host
must have \fIbmaptool\fR installed.
.RE
.PP
\-\-no-verify
.RS 2
Do not verify the image on the remote host (default).
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
\fIbmap-send\fR image.raw user@host:/data/image.raw
.RS 2
Send the mapped ranges of image.raw to /data/image.raw and its bmap to
/data/image.raw.bmap.
.RE
.PP
\fIbmap-send\fR \-\-full image.raw user@host:/data/image.raw
.RS 2
Send the complete image with scp.
.RE
.PP
\fIbmap-send\fR \-\-zero-holes image.raw root@host:/dev/sdX
.RS 2
Write the mapped ranges to a block device and zero its holes remotely.
.RE
.PP
\fIbmap-send\fR \-\-verify image.raw user@host:/data/image.raw
.RS 2
Send the mapped ranges and run "\fIbmaptool\fR verify /data/image.raw.bmap" on
the remote host afterwards.
.RE
.PP
\fIbmap-send\fR \-P 2222 \-y image.raw user@host:/data/
.RS 2
Send the mapped ranges into the remote /data/ directory using port 2222 and
without prompting if the source image mtime changed.
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
0 on success, 1 on error or when the transfer is aborted.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bmaptool (1)
